  "headline_finding": {
    "claim": "The deterministic DTL aggregate-authority invariant scores 0.8438 on CROSS_RAIL_SPLIT whether or not the family was in training (0.8438 seen), because it is arithmetic over the delegated grant - holdout-independent by construction, so this is an identity rather than a measurement that happened to come out equal. A model WITHOUT a cross-rail view reaches only 0.1719 held out: one leg genuinely looks like ordinary spending, so the signal is not in the transaction. That gap against hybrid ML's 0.8281 is wider than the 95% intervals at n=64, so it is a real separation and not sampling noise. Given DTL aggregate features, hybrid ML reaches 0.8281 held out against 0.8438 seen - a difference of 0.0157 that the 95% intervals at n=64 cannot resolve. The honest reading is that this run does not show the classifier degrading on an unseen family; it does NOT show that it never would, and a held-out result this close should not be presented as proven generalisation. The asymmetry is the point and it does not depend on the sample size: the invariant's two numbers are equal BY CONSTRUCTION, while the classifier's are two separate measurements that happen to be close on this run.",
    "cross_rail_split_recall_when_family_held_out": {
      "rules_only": 0.3906,
      "per_rail_ml": 0.1719,
      "ml_without_dtl": 0.1719,
      "hybrid_dtl_ml": 0.8281,
      "dtl_invariant_only": 0.8438,
      "note": "Every learned model scores each transaction in isolation, so a cross-rail leg looks ordinary to it. The deterministic DTL invariant compares AGGREGATE exposure against the delegated ceiling and is therefore holdout-independent: it does not need to have seen the attack family before."
    },
